When a change to an element’s spellcheck, readonly, disabled, type, or
contenteditable attribute turns checking off for text under it, Ladybird
drops that text’s spelling markers. But when checking turns back on,
Ladybird starts no check: A script turns it on, not the user — and a
check that a page could start at will would let it test any words it
likes against the user’s dictionary.

For the same reason, Blink starts no check when checking turns on, in
IdleSpellCheckController::RespondToChangedEnablement(). But WebKit’s
HTMLTextFormControlElement::effectiveSpellcheckAttributeChanged() clears
a text control’s markers when checking turns off, and it checks the
whole control again once checking turns back on for it.
